# `do TASK`: the AI suggests commands for a task, they are shown, and they
# run only after `y`. beehive's fake backend makes the task itself the plan
# (commands separated by ";").
@ai
$& do "mkdir /photos; echo hi > /photos/a.txt; ls /photos"
> y
$wait
Plan from the fake backend (no LLM).
  1. mkdir /photos
  2. echo hi > /photos/a.txt
  3. ls /photos
~ ^Run them\? y: yes, any other key: no
~ ^\$ mkdir /photos$
~ ^\$ echo hi > /photos/a\.txt$
~ ^\$ ls /photos$
~ a\.txt
done: 3 of 3 ran
# Anything but y runs nothing.
$& do rm /photos/a.txt
> n
$wait
  1. rm /photos/a.txt
~ ^Run it\? y: yes, any other key: no
nothing was run
$ cat /photos/a.txt
hi
# Some commands a plan never runs, even with y; a command that fails stops the rest.
$& do "reboot; cat /nothing.txt; echo never > /photos/b.txt"
> y
$wait
  1. reboot   (will not run: stops the machine)
skipped: reboot (stops the machine)
~ ^\$ cat /nothing\.txt$
~ no such file or directory
stopped: 'cat /nothing.txt' did not work (0 of 3 ran)
$ ls /photos
!~ b\.txt
$ do
usage: do TASK   (e.g. do make a folder photos with a file readme.txt in it)
# The log has the outcome of each request, never the task or the plan.
$ log 30
~ "ev":"ai","kind":"do","result":"done"
!~ photos
